---
<Base title="Checkpoints, torn pages and the log" description="The operator’s side of storage and durability: how often to checkpoint and what it costs, how each engine survives a torn page, when a flush can be trusted, what keeps the log from being reused, how long a restart takes, and four incidents read from the signals, for PostgreSQL, InnoDB, SQL Server and SQLite.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design storage-essay checkpoints-log">
    <StorageDefs />
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Checkpoints, torn pages and the log</h1><TitleLine type="Companion" version="PostgreSQL 18, with InnoDB (MySQL 8.4), SQL Server and SQLite" /></header>
<ArticleBody depth={3}>

    <p>The deep dive, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Storage and durability</a>, showed why a commit writes a log record and leaves the page for later. Writing pages later leaves three jobs for whoever runs the database. The pages have to reach disk often enough that recovery stays short. A page caught half-written by a power cut has to be repaired. And the log, written on every commit, has to be retired once nothing needs it.</p>
    <p>Each job has a setting, a cost and a way to fail. This article goes through them in that order, then through the trust all three place in the storage, and the time a restart takes. Four incidents at the end use the signals together. PostgreSQL 18 is the worked example, with InnoDB (MySQL 8.4), SQL Server and SQLite where they differ. The measurements come from PostgreSQL 18.6 on one laptop and show patterns, not numbers to expect.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="checkpoints"><h2 id="checkpoints">What a checkpoint costs</h2>
      <p>A <strong>checkpoint</strong> makes sure every page changed before a log position is in the data files, then records that position as the place crash recovery will start. Log before it is no longer needed for recovery. Every engine trades three costs here. Recovery time grows with the log since the last checkpoint. Page writes grow with how often checkpoints run, because a page that changes again after one has to be written again. And in PostgreSQL, log volume grows with checkpoint frequency too, for a reason the section on torn pages explains.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL checkpoints in rounds. Every 5 minutes (<code>checkpoint_timeout</code>), or sooner once the log grows towards <code>max_wal_size</code> (1 GB), it picks a position, writes every page dirtied before it, and <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>records the position</a>. It spreads the writes over most of the interval (<code>checkpoint_completion_target</code>, 0.9), so the disk is not hit all at once.</p>
      <p>InnoDB moves the position continuously. Its <a href={`${mysql}innodb-checkpoints.html`}>fuzzy checkpointing</a> flushes dirty pages in small batches, and the checkpoint advances behind the oldest page still dirty. Its redo log has a fixed capacity, so the position can’t fall too far behind. When writes fill the log faster than <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>flushing keeps up</a>, a <strong>sharp checkpoint</strong> forces dirty pages out, and writes stall until space is free.</p>
      <p>SQL Server’s <a href={`${mssql}logs/database-checkpoints-sql-server`}>indirect checkpoints</a>, the default for databases created since SQL Server 2016, also work continuously. You give a target recovery time, 60 seconds by default, and a background writer keeps the dirty pages few enough to meet it. SQLite in WAL mode checkpoints when the log reaches 1,000 pages.</p>
      <p>The figure runs rounds and the continuous model over the same forty price changes, for products on pages 10 to 15. Choose the model and how often it acts, then compare the replay, the page writes and, for PostgreSQL, the full-page images.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="checkpoints"
        number={1}
        title="Checkpoints in rounds and continuously"
        alt="Six pages in shared buffers, marked clean or dirty, above a grid of log records labelled with their pages. C marks a checkpoint."
        caption="Checkpoints here are instant and counted in records; real ones are timed and spread out. The continuous model flushes the pages that have been dirty longest after every batch, which is a simplification of InnoDB’s pacing. InnoDB doesn’t log full-page images, so the continuous model counts none."
      />
      <p>In rounds, a checkpoint every 5 records leaves 5 records to replay after the crash, at the price of 30 page writes and 35 full-page images. A checkpoint every 20 records leaves 20 to replay, with 6 writes and 12 images. Rare checkpoints cost less in writes and log, and more in recovery time.</p>
      <p>The continuous model writes fewer pages for the same changes, 24 against 30 at every 5 records, because a page that is still changing stays in memory until it has been dirty a while. But some log always remains to replay, because the position trails the oldest dirty page: 7 records against 5. At every 20 records the two models agree, because by then every dirty page is old enough to write.</p>

      <h3 id="checkpoint-settings">Checkpoint settings</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Checkpoint settings by engine"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>What starts a checkpoint</th><th>Settings that matter, with defaults</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Time, or log size</td><td><code>checkpoint_timeout</code> (5 min), <code>max_wal_size</code> (1 GB), <code>checkpoint_completion_target</code> (0.9), <code>min_wal_size</code> (80 MB), <code>checkpoint_warning</code> (30 s)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB</td><td>Nothing in particular: flushing is continuous and speeds up as the log fills or the pool gets dirty</td><td><code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code> (100 MiB), <code>innodb_max_dirty_pages_pct</code> (90), <code>innodb_adaptive_flushing_lwm</code> (10), <code>innodb_io_capacity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>A target recovery time, continuously; older databases checkpoint automatically by an estimate of recovery time</td><td><code>TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME</code> (60 seconds), or the server’s <code>recovery interval</code> when the target is 0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite, WAL mode</td><td>Log size</td><td><code>PRAGMA wal_autocheckpoint</code> (1,000 pages); manual modes PASSIVE, FULL, RESTART and TRUNCATE</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3 id="choosing-interval">Choosing the interval</h3>
      <p>Start from a recovery-time target and work backwards. Say a restart may spend 30 seconds replaying. Replay rate times that time gives the most log a crash may leave to replay. At the 169 MiB a second measured in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#recovery-time">Storage and durability</a>, that is about 5 GiB. Treat that as a ceiling: the rate was measured with the log in the operating system’s cache. After a reboot, or with replayed pages read from a slow volume, the same 5 GiB could take several times as long, so measure your own rate on a cold copy before sizing to it.</p>
      <p>Then find the setting that bounds the log to replay. In PostgreSQL the worst case is close to <code>max_wal_size</code>, because a crash just before a checkpoint finishes replays from where the previous one began. In InnoDB it is the redo log capacity, because the checkpoint can trail the end of the log by at most that much. SQL Server asks for the target directly. Leave room below the ceiling: a checkpoint the disks can’t finish in time lets the log, and the replay, grow past PostgreSQL’s soft limit.</p>
      <p>Figure 2 shows what a checkpoint does to a running server. It plots a recorded run, sampled once a second: how fast the log was written, and how many buffers were dirty. The shaded bands are three checkpoints, from start to finish. For the experiment, <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> was lowered to its minimum, 30 seconds, so several cycles fit in two minutes.</p>
      <figure class="ws-figure" aria-labelledby="ws-cycle-title">
        <div class="ws-head"><span class="ws-number">Figure 2</span><p class="ws-name" id="ws-cycle-title">PostgreSQL: log written and dirty buffers through three checkpoints</p></div>
        <div class="ws-board paper-surface">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 360 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="ws-cycle-title" aria-describedby="ws-cycle-desc">
            <desc id="ws-cycle-desc">Two charts sharing a time axis from 0 to 120 seconds. Three shaded bands mark checkpoints running from about 30 to 57, 60 to 87 and 90 to 117 seconds. The upper line, log written per second, starts near 5.5 megabytes per second, falls to under 3 by 29 seconds, jumps back above 5 when the first checkpoint starts, and repeats a smaller rise and decay at 60 and 90 seconds. The lower line, dirty buffers, climbs from near zero to almost all 16,384 buffers by 30 seconds, falls once the first checkpoint begins writing, and then stays between about 10,000 and 12,000.</desc>
            {checkpoints.map(([a, b]) => <rect class="ws-band" x={x(a)} y="26" width={x(b) - x(a)} height="228" />)}
            <text class="ws-band-label" x={x(30) + 4} y="236">checkpoint</text>
            <text class="ws-band-label" x={x(30) + 4} y="248">writing</text>
            <text class="ws-panel" x="44" y="18">Log written, MB per second</text>
            {[0, 2, 4, 6].map(v => <g><line class="ws-grid" x1="44" x2="348" y1={walY(v)} y2={walY(v)} /><text class="ws-tick" x="38" y={walY(v) + 4} text-anchor="end">{v}</text></g>)}
            <path class="ws-wal" d={walPath} />
            <text class="ws-panel" x="44" y="148">Dirty buffers</text>
            {[0, 8192, 16384].map(v => <g><line class={v === 16384 ? 'ws-grid ws-limit' : 'ws-grid'} x1="44" x2="348" y1={dirtyY(v)} y2={dirtyY(v)} /><text class="ws-tick" x="38" y={dirtyY(v) + 4} text-anchor="end">{v === 0 ? '0' : v === 8192 ? '8k' : '16k'}</text></g>)}
            <path class="ws-dirty" d={dirtyPath} />
            {ticks.map(t => <text class="ws-tick" x={x(t)} y="272" text-anchor="middle">{t}</text>)}
            <text class="ws-tick" x="348" y="286" text-anchor="end">seconds</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Recorded on a laptop: PostgreSQL 18.6, pgbench at 400 transactions a second, 128 MB of shared buffers (16,384 pages), checkpoints every 30 seconds. A manual checkpoint ran just before the first sample, which is why the log rate also starts high.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Two patterns repeat. The dirty count rises until a checkpoint starts writing, then holds steady while the checkpointer and background writer clean pages about as fast as the workload dirties them. And the log rate jumps at every checkpoint start, from under 3 MB a second to between 4 and 5.5, then decays over the next 20 seconds, with no change in the workload. The jump is full-page images, which the section on torn pages explains: right after a checkpoint, almost every change is the first to its page.</p>
      <p>So a periodic rise in log volume and write I/O, lined up with checkpoint starts, is normal. What deserves attention is commit latency rising at the same moments. To see what checkpointing too often costs, the same two-minute workload ran again with <code>max_wal_size</code> lowered from 1 GB to 64 MB.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The same workload with two values of max_wal_size">
        <table>
          <caption>The same two-minute workload, run once with each setting, on one laptop. The counts are of checkpoints started.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Reading</th><th><code>max_wal_size = 1GB</code></th><th><code>max_wal_size = 64MB</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Checkpoints started by the timer / by log size</td><td>4 / 0</td><td>0 / 13</td></tr>
            <tr><td>“Checkpoints are occurring too frequently” warnings</td><td>0</td><td>13</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pages written by checkpointer + background writer</td><td>34,165 + 8,140</td><td>47,262 + 33</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log written</td><td>442 MB</td><td>421 MB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Full-page images</td><td>55,653</td><td>52,475</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average transaction latency (pgbench)</td><td>1.90 ms</td><td>2.62 ms</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Every checkpoint became one started by log size, 6 to 10 seconds after the last, and latency rose by about a third. Page writes rose by only about 12%, and the log hardly changed, because this workload scatters its updates over 33,000 pages: nearly every change hit a page not yet copied, at either interval. Where the same pages change many times a minute, frequent checkpoints multiply full-page images instead.</p>
      <p>To size InnoDB’s redo log, sample its position twice and divide the difference by the seconds between: that is redo written per second. The capacity divided by it is how long the log lasts before flushing must catch up. A server writing an invented 60 MiB a second would use the default 100 MiB in under 2 seconds. <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>Adaptive flushing</a> starts at 10% of the capacity and grows aggressive at 75%, so choose a capacity that covers the longest burst you want to absorb, and keep the gap between the two positions well below 75% of it. MySQL 8.4 can resize the log while running.</p>
      <Sql code={innodbLsn} label="Read InnoDB's redo log and checkpoint positions" />
      <p>The PostgreSQL series works the same sizing in detail in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/#max-wal-size">WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</a>, with the rule that turns <code>max_wal_size</code> into checkpoint spacing and the log line each checkpoint writes.</p>

      <h3 id="checkpoint-symptoms">Checkpoint symptoms</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Checkpoint symptoms and what to change"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What to change</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL’s log says checkpoints are occurring too frequently</td><td>Log size is starting checkpoints closer together than <code>checkpoint_warning</code></td><td>Raise <code>max_wal_size</code>, then check worst-case replay against your target</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Latency rises at regular intervals</td><td>Checkpoint writes, and image-heavy log, competing with queries and commits</td><td>Give the I/O more capacity, or lengthen the interval if recovery time allows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB writes stall now and then when busy</td><td>The redo log is nearly full and a sharp checkpoint is forcing pages out</td><td>Raise <code>innodb_redo_log_capacity</code>; check <code>innodb_io_capacity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQL Server write I/O high and steady after setting a short target</td><td>The background writer is keeping very few pages dirty to meet the target</td><td>Lengthen <code>TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME</code> if recovery time allows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The SQLite <code>-wal</code> file keeps growing</td><td>A reader is open across checkpoints, so none can finish</td><td>Find the long reader, or run a RESTART or TRUNCATE checkpoint in a gap between readers</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="torn-pages"><h2 id="torn-pages">Torn pages</h2>
      <p>A <strong>torn page</strong> is a page that was only partly written when the power failed. A database writes a whole page at a time, but a drive only promises to write each sector whole, traditionally 512 bytes. That makes a PostgreSQL page 16 sectors and an InnoDB page 32, and the power can fail between any two of them.</p>
      <p>A log record that says “change these bytes” can’t be applied to a page that is half old and half new, so an engine needs a whole copy of the page somewhere. PostgreSQL’s way is <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-FULL-PAGE-WRITES`}><code>full_page_writes</code></a>, on by default. The first time a page changes after a checkpoint, the log record for that change carries a <strong>full-page image</strong>, a copy of the whole page. Watch a page write fail partway, with the setting on and then off.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="torn-page"
        number={3}
        title="PostgreSQL: a torn page and its full-page image"
        alt="Page 12 as sixteen 512-byte sectors, in shared buffers and in the table file, with its WAL record below."
        caption="New sectors are striped; old ones are plain. Drives don’t promise to write sectors in order; the drawing shows them in order for clarity."
      />
      <p>With the image in the log, recovery doesn’t need the torn page at all. It copies the image over it and replays later changes on top. One image per checkpoint is enough: recovery always starts at the last checkpoint, so the first record it meets for a page is the one carrying the image.</p>
      <p>With the setting off, the record holds only the change, and recovery has no clean copy. If data checksums are on, the torn page fails its check and recovery stops with an invalid-page error. Without them, the new LSN in the page’s first sector makes it look up to date, so recovery skips the record and the tear goes unnoticed.</p>
      <p>InnoDB keeps the copy somewhere else. Before it writes a page to its place in the data file, it writes the page to a <a href={`${mysql}innodb-doublewrite-buffer.html`}>doublewrite buffer</a>, on by default. If the write is torn, recovery finds a good copy there and then applies the redo log. SQLite never overwrites a page it can’t restore: the rollback journal holds the original, and in WAL mode the main file changes only at a checkpoint, which keeps the log until the file is flushed.</p>
      <p>SQL Server keeps no copy. Its <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/alter-database-transact-sql-set-options">page checksum</a>, on by default, detects a torn page when it is next read, raises error 824 and records the page in <code>msdb.dbo.suspect_pages</code>. Repair is a restore of that page from backup, or a rebuild if only index pages were hit. In an availability group, <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/failover-clusters/automatic-page-repair-availability-groups-database-mirroring">automatic page repair</a> fetches a clean copy from a replica.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where each engine keeps the clean copy of a page"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Where the clean copy lives</th><th>When it is written</th><th>What it costs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>A full-page image in the log</td><td>At the first change to a page after a checkpoint</td><td>Log volume, up to 8 kB per page per checkpoint</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB</td><td>The doublewrite buffer</td><td>Every time a page is flushed</td><td>A second write of every page flushed, to a sequential area</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>Nowhere: the checksum detects the tear</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>A page restore, or a replica in an availability group</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite, rollback journal</td><td>The journal holds the original page</td><td>Before the page is changed</td><td>An extra file write and flushes at every commit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite, WAL mode</td><td>The log still holds the frames</td><td>At commit; the log is kept until the checkpoint is flushed</td><td>Checkpoint writes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The protection can sometimes be turned off. ZFS prevents partial page writes, so <code>full_page_writes</code> can be off on it. InnoDB’s <code>innodb_doublewrite</code> can be off, or set to detect only. Turning either off is a decision to rely on the storage writing a page atomically. If you cannot show that it does, leave it on and accept the cost.</p>

      <h3 id="write-cost">What one change writes</h3>
      <p>Raising P7’s price changes two bytes. Tallying what the engines write for it, with default settings and no index touched, shows why write volume is so much larger than the data changed.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL:</strong> a WAL record for the update and one for the commit. If this is page 12’s first change since the last checkpoint, the update record carries an 8 kB full-page image. Later, the checkpointer or background writer writes page 12, another 8 kB. Each index whose entry changes adds the same again.</li>
        <li><strong>InnoDB:</strong> redo records for the row and for its undo record, and a binary log event. Later, page 12 is written twice, 16 kB to the doublewrite buffer and 16 kB to its place, and the undo page is written too.</li>
        <li><strong>SQL Server:</strong> a log record holding the old and new values, and later one 8 kB page write. Nothing guards against a tear.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>So the two bytes cost at least 16 kB in PostgreSQL after a checkpoint and over 32 kB in InnoDB. Checkpoint spacing, the number of indexes and the row’s page decide how much of that each change pays.</p>

      <h3 id="checksums">Checksums</h3>
      <p>Detecting damage is a separate job from repairing it. A checksum stored with each page tells the engine that a page is wrong; only a clean copy fixes it. A checksum also catches damage that is not a tear, such as a bit flip on the drive, which no full-page image or doublewrite can repair.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s data checksums are on by default for clusters created by <code>initdb</code> in version 18, and it counts failures in <code>pg_stat_database.checksum_failures</code>. InnoDB’s <code>innodb_checksum_algorithm</code> defaults to <code>crc32</code>. SQL Server’s <code>PAGE_VERIFY CHECKSUM</code> is the default. SQLite does not checksum database pages by default; its WAL frames carry checksums, and an optional <a href={`${sqlite}cksumvfs.html`}>checksum extension</a> adds a per-page one.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="log-volume"><h2 id="log-volume">The log as a resource</h2>
      <p>The log is written on every commit, shipped to replicas and archives, and kept until nothing needs it. Four things set how much of it there is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>How many pages a change touches, because every changed page is logged and every index is more pages.</li>
        <li>How many changes there are, and how wide their rows.</li>
        <li>In PostgreSQL, the full-page images after each checkpoint, which <code>wal_compression</code>, off by default, can shrink.</li>
        <li>Rewrites that give back space, such as PostgreSQL’s <code>VACUUM FULL</code>, which write and log the whole table again.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The rate is worth knowing for your own server, because it sets how fast an archive or replica has to keep up. In the local run the log grew by 442 MB in two minutes at 400 transactions a second, about 9.5 kB per transaction of five short statements, mostly full-page images.</p>

      <h3 id="retention">What keeps the log</h3>
      <p>Log that recovery no longer needs can be reused, unless something else still needs it.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What keeps log files and what happens when they pile up"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Log</th><th>Kept until</th><th>If it piles up</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL WAL</td><td>A checkpoint has passed it and, with archiving on, the archive command has copied it</td><td>Files <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>accumulate</a> while archiving falls behind, past <code>max_wal_size</code>, until the disk fills</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL WAL held by a replication slot</td><td>The slot’s consumer, a standby or a logical replication client, has confirmed it</td><td>No limit by default: <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-replication.html`}><code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code></a> is -1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB redo log</td><td>The checkpoint has passed it</td><td>It can’t grow past its capacity, so a sharp checkpoint stalls writes instead</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MySQL binary log</td><td>30 days have passed (<code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code>), whether or not anything needs it</td><td>A busy server can fill its disk; a replica offline past expiry can’t continue</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQL Server transaction log</td><td>In the full recovery model, a <a href={`${mssql}logs/the-transaction-log-sql-server`}>log backup</a> has copied it; also the oldest active transaction, replication and availability-group replicas</td><td>The file grows; once it can’t, writes fail with error 9002</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite <code>-wal</code> file</td><td>A checkpoint has copied its frames into the database</td><td>The file grows, and keeps its size afterwards unless truncated</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>SQL Server names the reason directly: <code>log_reuse_wait_desc</code> in <code>sys.databases</code> reads <code>LOG_BACKUP</code>, <code>ACTIVE_TRANSACTION</code>, <code>REPLICATION</code>, <code>AVAILABILITY_REPLICA</code> or another cause. A database in the full recovery model with no log backups scheduled is the classic case: the log grows until the disk fills. Without accelerated database recovery, a long transaction holds the log too; with it, the log can be truncated past the transaction.</p>
      <Sql code={sqlServerLog} label="SQL Server: what each database's log is waiting for" />
      <p>PostgreSQL gives no single reason, so you ask. To see the commonest cause, the local server was restarted with archiving on, an archive command that always fails, and <code>max_wal_size</code> at 256 MB, then given 25 seconds of unthrottled load.</p>
      <Sql code={retention} label="PostgreSQL: archive state and what slots hold back" />
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="pg_wal while archiving fails and after it is repaired">
        <table>
          <caption>Observed in the local run. <code>max_wal_size</code> was 256 MB throughout; the starting size is segments left for reuse by earlier runs with a 1 GB limit.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Moment</th><th><code>pg_wal</code> size</th><th>Segments waiting to archive</th><th><code>failed_count</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Before the workload</td><td>576 MB</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>After 25 s of writes, archiving failing</td><td>2,080 MB</td><td>129</td><td>37</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Archive command repaired, archive caught up</td><td>2,096 MB</td><td>0</td><td>42</td></tr>
            <tr><td>After the next checkpoint</td><td>256 MB</td><td>0</td><td>42</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Two things are easy to miss. The log grew to eight times <code>max_wal_size</code> without any error; the checkpoint log lines showed it instead, each recycling nothing. And repairing the archive freed nothing by itself: the backlog was archived within 20 seconds, but the files stayed until the next checkpoint removed them.</p>
      <p>An abandoned replication slot looks the same, and <code>pg_replication_slots</code> shows it inactive with <code>wal_status</code> <code>extended</code>. Setting <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> caps what a slot may hold, and a slot that passes it is marked lost, so its consumer needs a fresh copy. That trades a replica for the primary’s disk, a choice the PostgreSQL series weighs in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/#retention">Replication in PostgreSQL</a>.</p>
      <p>Whatever holds the log, fix the cause, not the files. Never delete log files by hand: recovery and replicas may need them, and a missing one can leave the server unable to start.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="trust"><h2 id="trust">Trusting the flush</h2>
      <p>Every guarantee so far rests on the operating system saying that a flush has finished. The deep dive’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#flush">flush path</a> showed a drive that reports data safe while it sits in volatile memory. Between the database and durable storage there can be several layers, each able to lose data.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Layers where flushed data can be lost and what protects them"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What loses data there</th><th>What protects it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Database buffer</td><td>A crash of the server process, or anything worse</td><td>The commit waits for the flush</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Operating-system cache</td><td>A kernel panic or power loss</td><td><code>fsync</code> before replying</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Controller cache</td><td>Power loss, if the cache has no battery</td><td>A battery or flash-backed cache, or a write-through setting</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Drive cache</td><td>Power loss, if the drive acknowledged the flush early</td><td>Power-loss protection, or the cache turned off</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Virtual or network volume</td><td>Whatever the hypervisor or storage service does before it acknowledges a write</td><td>The provider’s documented meaning of an acknowledged write</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Read the provider’s documentation for the volume type you use: local instance storage, for example, may disappear when an instance stops, whatever it acknowledged.</p>
      <p>A flush can also fail. If the operating system could not write a page back, a retry may report success even though the data was dropped. PostgreSQL treats this as fatal: with <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-error-handling.html`}><code>data_sync_retry</code></a> off, the default, a failure to flush the data files raises a PANIC, and the server restarts and recovers from the log.</p>
      <p>Network file systems add a different risk. SQLite depends on file locks to keep two writers apart, and its documentation warns that locking is <a href={`${sqlite}howtocorrupt.html`}>often broken on network file systems</a>, so two processes can write at once and corrupt the file. Keep a SQLite database on local storage.</p>

      <h3 id="testing">Testing a volume</h3>
      <p>Counting flushes gives a rough test for a spinning disk. At 7,200 rpm it turns 120 times a second, so it can complete at most about 120 flushes a second, and a result in the thousands means a cache is answering. The test fails for SSDs: an honest drive with power-loss protection can flush thousands of times a second.</p>
      <p>Two tools measure what a flush costs. PostgreSQL’s <a href={`${docs}pgtestfsync.html`}><code>pg_test_fsync</code></a> times each flush method on your volume. <code>fio</code> with <code>--fsync=1</code> measures a write followed by a flush, at the block size and concurrency you choose. Neither tells you whether the flush is honest. What does is pulling the plug during a write test, which <code>diskchecker.pl</code> is built for, or the drive’s documented power-loss protection. On a managed service the provider chooses the drives, so these checks are theirs to make; the commit settings are still yours.</p>
      <p>The flush time is also the floor for commit latency. On the laptop behind the deep dive’s commit rates, a commit took 5 ms when the drive flushed for real, and 0.04 ms with PostgreSQL’s default method, because macOS acknowledges a plain flush before the drive has finished. SQLite offers <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_fullfsync`}><code>PRAGMA fullfsync</code></a> for the same reason.</p>
      <p>In production, watch the flush time against that baseline. PostgreSQL reports it in <code>pg_stat_io</code> once <code>track_wal_io_timing</code> is on, and sessions waiting on it show <code>WalSync</code>; the PostgreSQL series reads those columns in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/#wal-flush-timing">WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</a>. InnoDB counts redo flushes in <code>Innodb_os_log_fsyncs</code>, and the Performance Schema times its log file I/O. SQL Server sessions waiting for a log flush show the <code>WRITELOG</code> wait. If a commit takes 5 ms and a flush on that volume takes 4 ms, the commit isn’t slow; the storage is.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="restart"><h2 id="restart">How long a restart takes</h2>
      <p>The four parts of an outage, and the measured replay, which checkpoint settings bound, are in the deep dive’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#recovery-time">Recovery time</a>. A long PostgreSQL replay logs its progress every 10 seconds, and the series reads a restart line by line in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/#reading-restart">WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</a>.</p>
      <p>Unfinished transactions cost differently in each engine. PostgreSQL pays nothing: their versions are simply invisible. InnoDB opens after replay and rolls them back in the background, and the manual warns that this rollback <a href={`${mysql}innodb-recovery.html`}>can take three or four times</a> as long as the transaction had run. Restarting doesn’t cancel it; <code>innodb_force_recovery</code> at 3 skips it, and is meant only for getting data out of a damaged server. So a MySQL server can be open and still slow, with new sessions meeting locks held by transactions being rolled back.</p>
      <p>SQL Server without <a href={`${mssql}accelerated-database-recovery-concepts`}>accelerated database recovery</a> undoes them too, and the time grows with the longest transaction active at the crash. With it, recovery takes about the same time however long the transaction ran, at the cost of keeping versions in the database. That is the main reason to turn it on for a database that runs long batch transactions.</p>
      <p>SQLite recovers on the first open after a crash, holding a lock that makes other connections wait. Nothing records it unless the application has registered a <a href={`${sqlite}c3ref/log.html`}><code>sqlite3_log()</code></a> callback, which then receives a recovery notice.</p>
      <p>The server then opens with the pages recovery touched, not the ones the workload needs. Warming the pool again is covered in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/#cold">The buffer pool</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="incidents"><h2 id="incidents">Four incidents</h2>
      <p>Each incident starts from what someone notices and works towards a mechanism. The order of the checks matters more than any one number: each step rules out a family of causes before the next.</p>

      <h3 id="slow-commits">Commits got slow</h3>
      <p>Requests that write are slower, and the time is in <code>COMMIT</code>. First, confirm the time is spent waiting on the log and not on locks. In PostgreSQL, sessions in <code>WalSync</code>, <code>WalWrite</code> or the lightweight lock <code>WALWrite</code> are waiting for the log; in SQL Server, <code>WRITELOG</code>. Sessions waiting for a synchronous standby (<code>SyncRep</code>) are a <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/#acknowledgements">replication</a> question, and other lock waits belong to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/#waiting">Understanding database performance</a>.</p>
      <p>If they are waiting for the log, compare flush time per call with your baseline. A flush that got slower under an unchanged workload points at the storage: a noisy neighbour on a shared volume, a cloud volume out of burst credits, a failing device. If the slowdown comes and goes, line it up with checkpoint starts: image-heavy log and checkpoint writes are competing with commit flushes. On InnoDB, check whether the checkpoint age sits near the redo capacity. On SQLite, which allows one writer at a time, slow commits are as often a long write transaction holding the lock.</p>

      <h3 id="long-restart">A restart that takes too long</h3>
      <p>The server crashed and still isn’t accepting connections. Read the startup log: PostgreSQL’s progress lines give the current log position, and the end is roughly where the log stood at the crash, at most about <code>max_wal_size</code> past the last completed checkpoint. If the position advances, recovery is working and the question is only time. If replay finished but the server still isn’t ready, look at what follows, such as the end-of-recovery checkpoint on slow storage. On InnoDB, check the error log for rollback progress; on SQL Server, the recovery messages in the error log give percentages for each phase.</p>
      <p>The lasting fix is to decide how long recovery may take and set checkpoints to match, and to keep transactions that change millions of rows out of the application.</p>

      <h3 id="log-filling">The log is filling the disk</h3>
      <p>Disk alerts point at the log. In PostgreSQL, ask three questions in order. Is archiving failing? <code>pg_stat_archiver</code> shows a rising <code>failed_count</code>. Is a slot holding the log? <code>pg_replication_slots</code> shows an inactive slot, or one far behind. Is <code>wal_keep_size</code> large? If none of these, the log is being written faster than checkpoints retire it. In SQL Server, read <code>log_reuse_wait_desc</code>.</p>
      <p>Repair the archive, or decide a dead slot’s consumer will be rebuilt and drop the slot, then expect the space back at the next checkpoint; running <code>CHECKPOINT</code> brings it forward. In SQL Server, take a log backup. Then alert on archive failures, inactive slots and log backups that stop.</p>

      <h3 id="history-growing">A table or undo history that keeps growing</h3>
      <p>A PostgreSQL table grows although its row count is steady, InnoDB’s history list length climbs, or SQL Server’s version store grows. All three are old versions that can’t be cleaned up, and the first question is the same: what is the oldest transaction or snapshot, and who owns it? Usually it is a report, a batch job, or a connection left idle inside a transaction. The deep dive’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#versions">table of version costs</a> names the view to read in each engine, and PostgreSQL’s case is worked through in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/#maintenance">Row versions, transactions, and vacuum</a>, including retained snapshots held by slots and prepared transactions.</p>
      <p>Once the oldest transaction ends, the space is reused but rarely returned. What each rewrite costs is set out in the deep dive’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#fill-factor">Free space and fill factor</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="summary"><h2 id="summary">What to watch, by engine</h2>
      <p>The signals, gathered for reference. Most are counters that only grow, so take two readings a known interval apart and divide the difference by the interval. Counters reset: MySQL’s at every start, and PostgreSQL’s after crash recovery, so a rate across a restart means nothing. Some signals exist only if set up beforehand: PostgreSQL’s <code>track_wal_io_timing</code>, a monitoring role that can read other sessions, and a <code>sqlite3_log()</code> callback in each SQLite application.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Storage signals in PostgreSQL, InnoDB, SQL Server and SQLite">
        <table class="ws-summary">
          <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>PostgreSQL 18</th><th>InnoDB (MySQL 8.4)</th><th>SQL Server</th><th>SQLite</th><th>A bad reading suggests</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Checkpoints</td><td>Server log with <code>log_checkpoints</code>; <code>num_timed</code>, <code>num_requested</code> in <code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code></td><td>Checkpoint age: <code>Innodb_redo_log_current_lsn</code> − <code>_checkpoint_lsn</code> against capacity</td><td><code>TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME</code> against write I/O</td><td><code>PRAGMA wal_checkpoint</code> result</td><td>Mostly started by log size, “too frequently” warnings, age near capacity, or checkpointed behind log</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Who writes pages</td><td><code>writes</code> by <code>backend_type</code> in <code>pg_stat_io</code></td><td><code>Innodb_buffer_pool_wait_free</code></td><td>Lazy writer and checkpoint page counters in <code>sys.dm_os_performance_counters</code></td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Query sessions writing pages, or waiting for free ones</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log rate</td><td><code>wal_bytes</code>, <code>wal_fpi</code> in <code>pg_stat_wal</code></td><td><code>Innodb_os_log_written</code></td><td>Log bytes flushed per second</td><td><code>-wal</code> file growth</td><td>A rise with unchanged traffic: new indexes, bulk changes, more full-page images</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log kept</td><td><code>pg_stat_archiver</code>; <code>pg_replication_slots</code>; size of <code>pg_wal</code></td><td>Binary log directory size</td><td><code>log_reuse_wait_desc</code></td><td><code>-wal</code> file size</td><td>Archiving failing, an abandoned slot, no log backups, a reader that never ends</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Log flush</td><td><code>wal</code> rows of <code>pg_stat_io</code>; waits <code>WalSync</code>, <code>WalWrite</code></td><td><code>Innodb_os_log_fsyncs</code>; redo file I/O time in the Performance Schema</td><td><code>WRITELOG</code> waits</td><td>Commit time in the application</td><td>Flush time up under the same workload: the storage is slower</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Old versions</td><td><code>n_dead_tup</code>; oldest <code>backend_xmin</code></td><td>History list length; oldest <code>INNODB_TRX</code></td><td>Version store size</td><td><code>-wal</code> size with checkpointed behind log</td><td>A long transaction or snapshot, or cleanup falling behind</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Page damage</td><td><code>checksum_failures</code> in <code>pg_stat_database</code></td><td>Checksum errors in the error log</td><td>Error 824; <code>msdb.dbo.suspect_pages</code></td><td>Errors from <code>PRAGMA integrity_check</code></td><td>A failing device or a write that was lost</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Startup lines; progress every 10 s</td><td>Recovery and rollback messages in the error log</td><td>Recovery phase messages in the error log</td><td>Recovery notices via <code>sqlite3_log()</code></td><td>Replay longer than your target; a large rollback after opening</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources"><h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
      <p>Checked on 17 and 18 October 2026. PostgreSQL outputs were recorded on a laptop with PostgreSQL 18.6. No MySQL or SQL Server instance was run; their names come from the manuals.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}>WAL Configuration</a> for checkpoint triggers, the warning and why log accumulates; <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html`}>WAL settings</a> for <code>full_page_writes</code>; <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-replication.html`}>replication settings</a>; <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-error-handling.html`}>error handling</a>; <a href={`${docs}monitoring-stats.html`}>The Cumulative Statistics System</a>; <a href={`${docs}wal-reliability.html`}>Reliability</a>; <a href={`${docs}pgtestfsync.html`}>pg_test_fsync</a>.</li>
        <li>MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual: <a href={`${mysql}innodb-checkpoints.html`}>Checkpoints</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-flushing.html`}>Buffer Pool Flushing</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-redo-log.html`}>Redo Log</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-doublewrite-buffer.html`}>Doublewrite Buffer</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-recovery.html`}>InnoDB Recovery</a>, <a href={`${mysql}binary-log.html`}>The Binary Log</a> and <a href={`${mysql}server-status-variables.html`}>Server Status Variables</a>.</li>
        <li>SQL Server: <a href={`${mssql}logs/database-checkpoints-sql-server`}>Database checkpoints</a>, <a href={`${mssql}logs/the-transaction-log-sql-server`}>The transaction log</a> and <a href={`${mssql}logs/troubleshoot-a-full-transaction-log-sql-server-error-9002`}>Troubleshoot a full transaction log</a>, <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/alter-database-transact-sql-set-options">page verify checksum</a>, <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/failover-clusters/automatic-page-repair-availability-groups-database-mirroring">automatic page repair</a> and <a href={`${mssql}accelerated-database-recovery-concepts`}>Accelerated database recovery</a>.</li>
        <li>SQLite: <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html`}>Write-Ahead Logging</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}atomiccommit.html`}>Atomic Commit</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}howtocorrupt.html`}>How To Corrupt An SQLite Database File</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}cksumvfs.html`}>the checksum extension</a> and <a href={`${sqlite}c3ref/log.html`}>the error log</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Storage and durability</a>: the mechanisms behind every setting here.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/buffer-pool/">The buffer pool</a>: sizing the cache and warming it after a restart.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</a>: PostgreSQL’s checkpoint and restart log lines, the <code>max_wal_size</code> trigger rule and flush timing.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/">Replication in PostgreSQL</a>: slots and the log they retain.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backups and recovery</a>: archiving, point-in-time recovery and what an incomplete archive costs.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
